              <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-gray-900 sm:text-3xl">Detections as code, written in Sigma</h3>
              <p class="mt-4 text-lg text-gray-600">Paste a Sigma rule, or start from the Rule Library: 47 ready-made rules for identity, endpoint, cloud, network, web and Kubernetes threats, written for the fields every source is normalized to. The 20 marked Recommended read data most projects already have, and one click adds them. Pick an interval between one minute and 24 hours; OneUptime parses and compiles the rule when you save it, so a broken rule fails in front of the person editing it rather than silently at 3 AM. Field names can be OneUptime columns, common Sigma spellings like <span class="font-mono text-sm text-gray-700">CommandLine</span> or <span class="font-mono text-sm text-gray-700">src_ip</span>, or any flattened key from the original payload. Group By, Distinct Count and a Match Count Threshold turn a rule into &ldquo;ten failed sign-ins from one IP&rdquo; or &ldquo;one IP against five accounts&rdquo;, with one detection per host, user or IP instead of one per event.</p>
              <p class="mt-4 text-lg text-gray-600">Sigma correlations join rules into what one event cannot show: failed sign-ins followed by a success from the same address, an account given admin rights within an hour of being created, or a sign-in from an address the account never used.</p>
