                <span>UEBA and behavioral baselining. Security event monitors compare against thresholds you set, and first-seen rules compare with up to 30 days of history, but nothing learns a baseline for a user or host, or scores its risk. Threat intel matches the lists and feeds you add; OneUptime curates no feed of its own.</span>
              </li>
              <li class="flex gap-3">
                <svg class="h-5 w-5 text-gray-300 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M6 18L18 6M6 6l12 12" /></svg>
                <span>An AI that closes cases or contains threats on its own. OneUptime AI reads, cites and suggests; a person sets the verdict, and a playbook&rsquo;s containment steps wait for approval by default.</span>
              </li>
              <li class="flex gap-3">
                <svg class="h-5 w-5 text-gray-300 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M6 18L18 6M6 6l12 12" /></svg>
                <span>Containment beyond Okta, CrowdStrike and Microsoft Defender. To act on any other product, a playbook sends a webhook or starts a workflow that you write.</span>
              </li>
              <li class="flex gap-3">
                <svg class="h-5 w-5 text-gray-300 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M6 18L18 6M6 6l12 12" /></svg>
                <span>A drop-in replacement for a large dedicated SIEM program. If that is what you are running, this sits alongside it &mdash; import its detections through a connector, send it what OneUptime finds, and let the detections that matter page like everything else.</span>
              </li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

